                 DIGITAL EQUIPMENT CORPORATION AND
                   SYNOPTICS COMMUNICATIONS, INC.
              DEMONSTRATE ATM AND ETHERNET INTEGRATION


MAYNARD, Mass. -- May 2, 1994 -- Digital Equipment Corporation and 
SynOptics Communications Inc. will demonstrate at NetWorld+INTEROP 
'94 next week the integration of Ethernet and Asynchronous Transfer 
Mode (ATM) local area networks (LANs).  Digital and SynOptics have 
cooperated in the development of an interoperable LAN emulation 
solution for Ethernet-to-ATM connectivity over Permanent Virtual 
Circuits (PVCs), based on key work being done in the ATM Forum LAN 
Emulation Group. The two companies will show an Alpha AXP 
workstation configured with Digital's ATM TURBOchannel adapter, and 
a DECstation 5000 with an Ethernet adapter communicating across a 
Digital ATM switch, a SynOptics LattisCell ATM switch, and a 
SynOptics EtherCell ATM switch. 

     ATM products that support LAN emulation enable customers to 
preserve their existing product and application investments as they 
migrate to ATM. LAN emulation supports a wide variety of protocols, 
including TCP/IP, DECnet, IPX, AppleTalk, LAT, and NetBIOS. 
Moreover, LAN emulation provides a LAN-like service over ATM by 
creating the appearance of a shared medium out of a connection-oriented, 
switched network. This service includes the ability to accommodate 
bursts of LAN traffic at link speeds.  

     "SynOptics strongly supports cooperation between vendors to 
provide best-of-breed solutions for customers," said Mike Goguen, 
SynOptics' Product Manager for ATM. "Today's LANs are inherently 
multivendor systems, and it is up to LAN vendors to work together 
to ensure smooth transitions to new technologies, such as ATM 
switching." 

     "One of the foremost concerns customers have regarding the 
adoption of ATM technology is their ability to migrate their 
current LANs and all associated applications to an ATM 
environment," said Pete Dunbeck, Digital's ATM Program Manager. 
"This technology demonstration at NetWorld+INTEROP '94 offers a 
solution that can accelerate the standardization of LAN emulation 
for multivendor interoperability."

     SynOptics Communications, Inc. is a leading vendor of 
networking products and management systems to organizations 
worldwide. SynOptics, founded in 1985, is headquartered in Santa 
Clara, California, and has more than 50 offices worldwide. 
SynOptics is publicly traded over the counter under the NASDAQ 
symbol SNPX. 

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems. Digital's scalable Alpha AXP 
platforms, storage, networking, software and services, together 
with industry-focused solutions from business partners, help 
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace.
