                   DIGITAL WINS $27-MILLION ORDER
              FROM U.S. DEPARTMENT OF VETERANS AFFAIRS


            Digital To Install 500 DECpc AXP/150 Systems
            With OpenVMS and Digital Standard M Software
                 At 98 VA Medical Centers Nationwide


MAYNARD, Mass. -- May 4, 1994 -- The U.S. Department of Veterans
Affairs (VA) has awarded Digital Equipment Corporation a $27-million
order to upgrade its computer systems at 98 medical centers 
nationwide to Alpha AXP technology.  Under the terms of the order, 
Digital will replace existing VAX systems with 500 clustered DECpc 
AXP/150 systems, beginning last month. 

     The VA will run its mission-critical patient databases and 
hospital information system (HIS) applications completely on Alpha 
AXP PCs using the OpenVMS operating system and Digital Standard M 
software.  Configured with 128MB of memory, three SCSI controllers, 
an EISA FDDI controller, and an EISA Ethernet controller, these 
systems are capable of high-capacity I/O and high performance.  
Individual hospital configurations will be comprised of either four, 
five, or six Alpha AXP PCs, configured in local area OpenVMSclusters 
supporting shadowed databases of 20GB and larger. 

     The VA was looking to downsize its computer systems to a 
platform with the price/performance level and support costs 
associated with 486-based PCs. Another concern was the ability to 
migrate existing HIS applications to a new platform, according to 
Robert E. Scott, deputy director, Birmingham (Alabama) Information 
Systems Center, which handles nationwide computer system procurement 
for the VA.

     In evaluating new platforms, the VA found that clusters of four 
to six DECpc AXP/150 systems provided faster performance than a 
large number of networked 486-based PCs.  The existing HIS 
applications, based on Digital Standard M software, could be 
migrated from the VAX systems to the Alpha AXP PC systems because 
both support the OpenVMS operating system.

     "No software modifications were required for either the 
operating environment or the program applications," said Scott.  
"The migration is a straightforward cut-over, with downtime 
amounting to less than 30 minutes."

     Another factor in the VA's choice, Scott explained, was the 
Alpha AXP systems' capability to also run the DEC OSF/1 and 
Microsoft Windows NT operating systems.  This provides the VA with 
the freedom to choose the operating environment best suiting its 
needs should its applications requirements change over time.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha AXP 
platforms, storage, networking, software and services, together 
with industry-focused solutions from business partners, help 
organizations compete and win in today's global marketplace. 
