                 HEAD OF DIGITAL RESEARCH DISCUSSES
                INFORMATION REVOLUTION AND TECHNOLOGY
                   IN TALK TO DIGITAL USERS GROUP


NEW ORLEANS -- May 10, 1994 -- Dr. Samuel H. Fuller, Digital 
Equipment Corporation's Vice President for Corporate Research, said 
today that the creation of vast new information systems through the 
convergence of previously distinct industries and dramatic advances 
in technology demonstrate that "the information revolution has only 
just begun."

     "The boundaries that once separated information industries such 
as computing, communications and consumer electronics are 
disappearing," Fuller told the spring meeting of the U.S. Chapter of 
the Digital Equipment Corporation Users Society (DECUS).  "From the 
Internet to mobile communications and interactive video, new 
partnerships, new alliances -- even new industries -- are being 
created almost every day.  All of this is being driven by quantum 
leaps in technology -- in performance, efficiency, connectivity and 
ease of use, to name just a few.

     "The result will be revolutionary changes in the way we 
deliver, receive, store, manipulate, share and respond to 
information," he said.  "And Digital is playing a leading role in 
this revolution."

     One example of the rapid emergence of this new environment, 
according to Fuller, is the Internet, which began as a network 
primarily linking scientific and academic interests and has now 
moved well beyond those origins.

     The introduction of the World-Wide Web and MOSAIC makes it much 
easier for users to identify and gain access to Internet files and 
databases.  Today, the Internet forms the basis of a new system of 
electronic commerce.  It links more than 2 million host computers on 
20,000 networks in 140 countries with more than 20 million users.  
And it is growing rapidly.  A new network connects to the Internet 
every 10 minutes.

     "As commercial uses of the Internet have expanded, Digital has 
been in the forefront in finding creative ways to use this 
technology to meet customer needs," said Fuller.

     For example, Digital is a sponsoring member of the newly formed 
CommerceNet consortium, an open, Internet-based communications 
highway for commerce.  It will include services and applications 
designed to make the Internet easy to use for commercial 
organizations requiring reliability, security and easy access for 
first-time users.

     Digital sees the Internet as a cost-effective way to work 
closely with customers and partners, Fuller said.  Digital is making 
its Alpha AXP computers available for remote access over the 
Internet.  Customers, potential customers and third-party software 
developers can log-on for free and see how their own applications 
would run in the new environment.

     Fuller said that Digital also is introducing comprehensive 
Internet Security Services, a combination of expert security 
consulting and software capabilities that help make private computer 
networks and databases more secure from intrusion from the Internet.

     In addition to its innovative work on the Internet, Digital 
Research is developing new technologies and applications that will 
produce further advances in products and services.  "Some people 
view researchers as men and women in white lab coats pursuing 
pie-in-the-sky science.  But at Digital, research is a very 
customer-oriented discipline," Fuller said.

     He cited several projects that Digital is working on that have 
tremendous potential as commercial applications:

     ATM (asynchronous transfer mode), the wave of the future in 
networking.  Digital is a leader in the development of this 
technology, which permits an unlimited number of users to have 
dedicated high-speed, high-throughput connections with each other 
and with high-performance network servers.  Among other things, it 
will provide seamless connections between local-area-networks and 
wide-area-networks.  Fuller said Digital expects global corporations 
to adopt ATM to support larger, more mobile user populations, 
multiple technologies, distributed computing and multimedia 
information services.

     Project Sequoia 2000.  Initiated by Digital in 1991, Sequoia 
2000 is a cooperative program with industry, government and 
universities to develop a system for storing and managing the 
incredible amounts of information that will be provided by new 
earth-monitoring satellites. When the satellites are in place later 
in this decade, they will generate more than one terabyte of data 
each day, or more than 300 terabytes a year.  "We must understand how 
to store, catalog, access and use this data in useful ways," said 
Fuller.  This research will lead to truly advanced computing for 
companies and industries that must manage huge amounts of information.

     The BERKOM Project.  This project, a partnership between German 
Telekom and Digital's campus-based Engineering Center in Germany, is 
focused on realizing the benefits of Multimedia Mail using 
Multimedia Collaboration.  The goal is virtual conferencing, where 
individuals could sit in front of their computers and, via real-time 
audio and video connections, communicate with their colleagues.  
"The many possible benefits include easy exchange of information, 
increased productivity and the ability to bring experts together to 
solve problems," said Fuller.

