             DIGITAL EQUIPMENT CORPORATION'S UNIX SYSTEM
               BREAKS FILE SERVICE PERFORMANCE BARRIER


         ...Establishes new record providing 50% improvement
        over industry's next fastest competitive systems with
                 AdvantageCluster File Server system


MAYNARD, Mass. -- May 18, 1994 - Digital Equipment Corporation today
announced that its AdvantageCluster Fileserver 3000 configuration 
provides over 51% greater throughput than Network Appliance's 
FAstserver, and more than 69% shorter response time than the 
SPARCcluster Model 4 from Sun Microsystems, the next fastest
systems in these two performance categories. This performance
breakthrough results from Digital's most recent testing using the 
SFS benchmark developed and administered by the System Performance
Evaluation Committee (SPEC.) The SFS benchmark test determines
throughput and response times for file services in UNIX networks. 

     "Our customers need cost-effective servers for handling very 
large file service loads with fast response times for many users," 
said John O'Keefe, vice president, UNIX System Marketing for 
Digital.  "The AdvantageCluster File Server 3000 system running 
under DEC OSF/1, Digital's UNIX implementation, delivers this high 
performance for users, makes it easy for system managers, and most 
of all is scalable beyond current configurations to keep up with our 
customers' growing demands for computing performance." 

     The AdvantageCluster Fileserver is a member of Digital's 
expanding AdvantageCluster product family of UNIX clusters, which 
provide industry-leading scalability and availability solutions. 

     The benchmark results are tabulated below for Digital's systems 
as well as those of other vendors offering file server systems.   
A graphical representation of the data is also available. 


     System			  Benchmark Performance


    			     NFS ops./sec.     Avg Response (msec.)

   DEC AdvantageCluster
   Fileserver 3000              4817                26.4

   Auspex NS6000
   Netserver                    2037                46.5

   HP 9000 H60                  1313                48.0

   IBM 7051 Power
   Network DataServer           2031                47.4
                                                                       
   SUN SPARCcenter 2000         2575                48.9
                                                                       
   SUN SPARCcluster 1
   Model 4                      3043                44.6

   Network Appliance
   FAserver                     3189                N/A     


     Network File Service throughput is measured in NFS operations 
per second, and is an indicator of the number of user requests for 
file services a system can handle over a period of time, and 
therefore the practical number of users a system will support based 
on file requests.  Response Time is measured in milliseconds, and is 
an indicator of the elapsed time during which users could expect to 
wait for data to be available to an application.  The higher the 
number of operations per second, the better the performance, while 
response time should virtually instantaneous.

     "Digital has been enhancing UNIX on its Alpha AXP family since 
the first release of the DEC OSF/1 operating system on these 
platforms," said O'Keefe.  "We have been the UNIX performance leader 
since March of 1993, and these results show that we are continuing 
that lead.  And Digital offers the most standards-compliant UNIX 
implementation in the industry, meeting over 99% of the standards 
submitted to X/Open Ltd. in the SPEC 1170 submission last December."

     The SPEC SFS benchmark Release 1.0 (formerly the LADDIS 
benchmark) measures two important network performance criteria, NFS 
throughput and Average NFS Response Time.  Results of the SPEC SFS 
benchmarking tests are filed with the System Performance Evaluation 
Committee before they can be made publicly available and are on file 
for any system for which SPEC benchmark results are published. Based 
on these published results, Digital's AdvantageCluster Fileserver 
3000 system exceeds the NFS throughput performance of the highest 
performing systems from any other vendor and offer the shortest 
average response times as well.  "The capabilities we have built 
into our AdvantageCluster Systems for UNIX users are second to 
none," said O'Keefe.  "We offer exceptional file server performance 
as demonstrated here, as well as high availability and extremely 
high compute performance configurations to meet the most compute-
intensive application needs of our customers."  

     The AdvantageCluster Fileserver 3000 is available now, with 
configurations starting at $272,000.

