       Major Announcements and Windows NT/Alpha AXP Exhibits,
   including Digital 2100 Server Model A500MP system, Highlighted

                          
Windows World, Atlanta, GA -- May 23, 1994 -- Digital Equipment 
Corporation, a leading worldwide supplier of Microsoft Windows NT 
solutions, systems and services, is showcasing offerings from its 
leading partners at Windows World/COMDEX. To underscore its 
commitment to the burgeoning Windows NT market and to build on its 
earlier Digital 2100 Server Model A500MP system introduction, 
Digital is featuring partner, software and customer announcements. 
Digital also is featuring product demonstrations involving high-
performance, workstation-class Digital Alpha AXP platforms.  
Independent software vendors and partners are joining Digital to 
demonstrate their specific solutions in Digital's booth #8222.

     "Digital's exhibits highlight the productivity gains and 
competitive advantages customers can achieve today through the rapid 
deployment of Windows NT client/server solutions on Digital's CISC- 
and RISC-based platforms," said Willy Shih, Digital's vice president 
for UNIX/Windows NT systems.  "Digital is committed to working with 
its partners to continue to provide industry-leading Windows based 
solutions."

     Digital also is announcing its joint effort to work with 
Microsoft Corporation, Saturn Corporation (Automotive) and GE Fanuc 
Automation NA, Inc. (GEF).  This results from Saturn Corporation's 
request that Digital, GEF and Microsoft team together to build next 
generation mainstream manufacturing information systems.  This next 
generation system will be based on Microsoft Windows NT client/
server operating system and GEF's Cimplicity factory automation and 
control software running on Digital's Alpha AXP client/server 
platforms.

     Also announcing their commitment to the Alpha AXP platform 
running Windows NT and immediate availability, in most cases, of 
their ported applications are Digital partners -- Cadkey, Inc., 
Effective Management Systems, Inc., Great Plains Software, 
Hypercube, Inc., Matra Datavision, Micrografx, Inc. and Wolfram 
Research Inc. 

     Digital also is introducing a new original equipment 
manufacturer incentive program enabling PC manufacturers to enter 
the emerging RISC PC market quickly and cost effectively.

     Digital panel speakers featured at Windows World include 
Storage Business Unit Vice President Charlie Christ and Multimedia 
Marketing Manager John Koger.  Christ's presentation involves the 
changing roles of original equipment manufacturers as standards and 
technologies continue to evolve in the marketplace.  Koger's 
Multimedia Technologies panel explores the Windows NT capabilities 
available with video compression technology.

